Z||000023||theogre||10-03-1999||06:00 PM||||I tried on of those after market air jobs with 2 blowers awhile back..... It was nice and noisy,,,, problem is tho that all the ones I've seen split a diaphram after about a year.......<P>went with some dual electric last time.... they wook good, but I miss the air sound.... Air is too much to spend for it only to last a year or so....<P>Truck horns would be nice, but you have to fool with 100psi system and a large tank.... been there, done that, not fun.<P>Part of the problem is location... stock horns are pretty burried. Maybe moving them near the nose opening would help.||||reg||1
Z||000024||batboy||10-03-1999||06:45 PM||||GM usually uses two horns, one high tone and one low tone. On my 87 SE, I removed the factory low tone horn and replaced it with one from a 80's Chevy pickup. I left the other Fiero high tone horn. Now it sounds much more obnoxious, but only slightly louder. The car does sound bigger now though. Lots of people would hear that little beep beep and know it's a little bitty car so they keep cutting you off or whatever stupid thing they're doing. Ogre's probably right, the Fiero horns are tucked up under all that plastic in the nose and maybe they're too muffled.||||reg||1
Z||000025||DaRkLoRD||10-03-1999||07:25 PM||||One of my friends drives an old Sunbird.. he got the horn replaced, but about a year ago it was hilarious.. ever heard the horns on those little motorbikes the Shriners drive? (in Canada at least..) Sounded just like that.. couldn't hear it if you were more than 50 feet away probably. haha<P>funniest sound I've ever heard from a car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I've been thinking about the horns on the Fiero as well, JC Whitney has some that might be suitable.. and some that wouldn't be.. (imagine a horn that played part of the "macarena" song.... ugh!)||||reg||10
